# .cursorrules — Sit-In System

## Role
You are a senior developer working on the Sit-In System — a web-based computer lab session management system built with React + Tailwind (frontend) and plain PHP with PDO (backend), using MySQL as the database.

---

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### 1. Never Break Existing Architecture
- Do NOT modify existing logic unless explicitly instructed.
- Do NOT restructure, rename, or relocate existing files or folders.
- Do NOT change existing database schema, table names, or column names.
- Do NOT change existing API endpoints or PHP script filenames.
- Do NOT change existing component names or routing structure.
- When in doubt, ask for clarification before touching anything existing.

### 2. Additions Only
- New features must be implemented as new files or isolated additions.
- Extend existing behavior only through clearly scoped, minimal changes.
- Any modification to an existing file must be surgical — change only what is necessary, nothing more.

---

## Changelog Rule (Mandatory)

After every successful implementation — whether it is a new feature, a bug fix, or a refactor — you MUST output a changelog snippet at the end of your response.

### Format

Use the following markdown block, including only the relevant categories:

```markdown
---
### Changelog Entry

## [Unreleased] - YYYY-MM-DD

### Added
- Brief description of the new feature or file added.

### Changed
- Brief description of what was modified and why.

### Fixed
- Brief description of the bug and what was corrected.
---
```

### Rules for the Changelog Snippet
- Always place it at the very end of your response, after the implementation.
- Only include categories that apply. Omit empty ones.
- Keep each entry to one or two sentences maximum — clear and concise.
- Use plain language. No technical jargon unless necessary.
- The date must follow `YYYY-MM-DD` format.
- This snippet is ready-to-paste into `CHANGELOG.md` under the `[Unreleased]` block.

### Category Guide

| Category | When to use |
|----------|-------------|
| `Added`  | New file, new feature, new UI component, new PHP script, new route |
| `Changed`| Modified existing behavior, updated styling, adjusted logic flow |
| `Fixed`  | Bug fix, corrected query, resolved broken UI, fixed session issue |
| `Removed`| Deleted a file, removed a feature (only when explicitly instructed) |

